<.docs_layout active_guide={@guide.slug}>
  <.guide_heading guide={@guide} />

  <section class="mt-10 space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight">Five series, in this order</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Series take <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs">chart-1</code>
      … <code class="rounded bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-xs">chart-5</code>
      in order, the same five hues in both modes, stepped for each surface. The order is what keeps
      neighbours apart for colour-blind readers - never reorder or add a sixth. Switch the theme to
      see the dark steps.
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 rounded-lg border border-base-300 p-6 sm:grid-cols-5">
      <div
        :for={
          {n, hue} <- [{1, "blue"}, {2, "orange"}, {3, "aqua"}, {4, "yellow"}, {5, "magenta"}]
        }
        class="space-y-2"
      >
        <div class="h-12 rounded-md" style={"background: var(--chart-#{n})"}></div>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs font-medium">chart-{n}</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">{hue}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight">The form follows the job</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      One number is a number card. A ranking is horizontal bars, longest first, labelled at their
      ends. Progress through states is one stacked bar. Change over time is a line, or bars for
      days. Every one is the same
      <.link navigate={~p"/docs/components/chart"} class="link link-primary font-medium">
        &lt;.chart&gt;
      </.link>
      with an ordinary Chart.js config.
    </p>
    <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
      <.number_card
        id="guide-sales"
        label="One number · number card"
        value="$42,000"
        trend={ChartPages.sales_trend()}
      >
        Margin $14,700 (35%)
      </.number_card>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3 class="card-title text-sm">Progress through states · one stacked bar</h3>
          <.chart
            id="guide-progress"
            label="Today's appointments"
            class="mt-2"
            config={ChartPages.progress_config()}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3 class="card-title text-sm">A ranking · horizontal bars, end labels</h3>
          <.chart
            id="guide-leaderboard"
            label="Sales by person, this week"
            class="mt-2"
            config={ChartPages.leaderboard_config()}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h3 class="card-title text-sm">Change over time · one series in colour</h3>
          <.chart
            id="guide-weeks"
            label="Sales this week and last week"
            class="mt-2"
            canvas_class="h-56"
            config={ChartPages.weeks_config()}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 space-y-4">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight">Quiet marks, numbers in text</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        Bars at most 24px with a 4px rounded end; 2px lines; a 2px gap between stacked parts; grid
        lines on the value axis only. The theme draws all of it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Labels, values and legends are text colours; the series colour sits on a key beside them.
      </li>
      <li>
        A legend for two or more series, none for one. Never a number on every point, never two
        y-axes.
      </li>
      <li>
        Every chart has a name and its numbers as a hidden table; tooltips add to that, they never
        hold the only copy of a value.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-sm">
      Components:
      <.link navigate={~p"/docs/components/chart"} class="link link-primary font-medium">
        Chart
      </.link>
      ·
      <.link navigate={~p"/docs/components/number-card"} class="link link-primary font-medium">
        Number card
      </.link>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 space-y-3" data-platform="ios">
    <h2 class="text-lg font-medium tracking-tight">iOS / SwiftUI</h2>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-base-300 bg-muted/40 px-4 py-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground">
      <p>
        Swift Charts draws the same forms: BarMark (stacked, with an
        <code class="font-mono text-xs">.annotation(position: .trailing)</code>
        for a ranking's labels), LineMark and AreaMark, SectorMark. Use the five chart colours in
        order, hide the value axis when every bar is labelled, and give each chart an
        <code class="font-mono text-xs">AXChartDescriptor</code>
        through <code class="font-mono text-xs">.accessibilityChartDescriptor</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</.docs_layout>
